1. XSS
    - cross-site scripting - is a vulnerability that allows an attacker to inject malicious JS code into a web page,
    which is then executed in the victim's browser.
    3 types of XSS:
        1) stored - malicious code stored on the server (DB)
        2) reflected - an attacker crafts malicious url and sends it to a victim, which opens the link and malicious
        script in it is executed immediately.
        3) DOM based - malicious actions are executed on the frontend
    how to avoid such vulnerability
        1) output escaping
        2) httpOnly
        3) content security policy
2. CSRF
    - cross-site request forgery - is an attack when malicious website tricks a user's browser to send a request to
    another trusted website where user is authenticated. it is a vulnerability of session-based auth. an attacker abuses
    authorized cookies that are sent automatically.
    to avoid this type of attack the following should be used:
        1) CSRF token (server initially generates csrf token and sends it to a client, which then stores this token and
         uses it later on in each request, and the main thing is that it is not automatic, frontend manually attaches
         this token to every request)
        2) same-site cookies (use same-site cookie flag to prevent it from being sent from alien origin)

3. SQL injection
    - it is a vulnerability where an attacker injects malicious sql code into a query, allowing them to manipulate the
    database. thus the attacker can bypass login, read other people's data, delete data, execute admin-actions.
    to prevent this u must use prepared statements / parameterized query.

4. password hashing
    - is the process of transforming a password into a fixed, irreversible value (hash) before storing it.
    a server does not maintain a user's password in its initial form, while encryption does, thus if database and the
    key were stolen, it is easy to decrypt all the passwords. For example laravel uses bcrypt or argon2, they both
    automatically add salt (random value) to password before hashing, so that even if there are identical passwords,
    they anyway will be different because of that salt.

5. CORS
    - cross-origin resource sharing - is a Browser security mechanism that defines whether a web page can read responses
    from a different origin.
    there are several headers that server can use:
        Access-control-allow-origin: https://frontend.com - defines what origin is able to read responses.
        Access-control-allow-credentials: true - defines whether cookies are permitted.
        Access-control-allow-methods: GET, PUT, POST - defines what methods are permitted.

6. rate limiting
    - it is a mechanism that restricts the number of requests a client can make to a server within a period of time.
    it is used to prevent:
        abuse (spam, api abuse, brute force)
        DoS
    if a client reaches the limit, he gets a 429 response - Too many requests.
    the number of a client requests can be restricted by a user's id / IP...

7. mass assignment
    - it is a process of assigning all the attributes from a user input to a model. It can lead to unwanted results,
    because attacker may include unexpected fields such as admin flag for example.
    to prevent such attack u should use laravel $fillable or $guarded to control what values can be written.
    use some validation, FormRequest, DTO.